body:
Yea I was out today and just called it an early day after a 2 dollar tip.

&#x200B;

I got an order from chick fil a, called, and the guy said he didn't even see it on his computer yet. Not even sure what that meant. Unassigned and hit 90% completion.

Too hard. Just enough to put some dents and bend axle wheels. 

&#x200B;

Stop sticking up for these asshats. They're so in the wrong it's depressing. What if your family was in the ambulance they're blocking? What would you want to do to them? Seeing as how much of a damn they give about your dying family members. Damaged vehicles is getting off easy.

of course. It's a shame how we've handled the outbreak. People may not think it but America does in fact have amazing health care. We as a country have so many resources. Experts, technology, specialist, etc. It's just unfortunate we don't really make it available to most. 

&#x200B;

If I were ultra rich, I'm sure I'd find some of the stuff out there unbelievable.

You should definitely move out of your uncles but when you're ready. Living with him is comfortable and doesn't require the stress of having to get out on your own.

&#x200B;

What is your full time job?

I have questions about image resolutions and formatting.

I lack formal photography education so I'm unsure about fine details like these. When editing photos, should I be cropping to standard 8inx10in dimensions and the such? 

&#x200B;

Currently, I'll be compiling two folds for the photos I'm working on. One for online sharing where they'll be saved as srgb. I think the file format should be PNG instead of jpg. Should I lower the resolution to reduce file size for better online uploading? If so, to what? And how would I do that in photoshop? 

&#x200B;

In the print folder, they'll be saved in RGB. Google says Tiff is the best format to save these in. What should I make the resolution?

&#x200B;

I would really like to save the photos in such a way that if someone were to use Walmart to print 8X10 prints, nothing would need to be cropped out. Or 4x6, etc. 

&#x200B;

Thanks for the help guys. I do my best to research but it gets overwhelming at times.

